LEAD = {
    "headline": "IN EVERYDAY CONSUMER GOODS, NINE OF EVERY TEN YOUTUBE VIDEOS AN AI ENGINE PUTS IN FRONT OF A SHOPPER CAME FROM AN INDEPENDENT CREATOR RATHER THAN A BRAND, AND A VIDEO'S VIEW COUNT PREDICTS GETTING PICKED AT ALMOST EXACTLY ZERO",
    "deck": "A new write-up landed on Tuesday of an analysis nobody has published.  Underneath it sit two much larger datasets that have been sitting in plain sight since March and June, and together they say the same thing: the signals that make brand video good at being a hit are uncorrelated with being chosen as a source.  The one signal that does correlate is the field nobody in the creative department writes.",
    "stamps": [
        ("NET INFLUENCER · 14 OCT", "https://www.netinfluencer.com/ai-is-recommending-products-through-creator-videos-most-brands-arent-making-research-shows/"),
        ("OTTERLY AI · 2 MAR", "https://otterly.ai/blog/youtube-ai-citation-study-2026/"),
        ("JELLYFISH · 25 JUN", "https://www.jellyfish.com/en-gb/news/creator-intelligence-updates/"),
        ("SOCIAL MEDIA TODAY · 13 OCT", "https://www.socialmediatoday.com/news/youtube-rolls-out-ai-search-chatbot-to-all-us-users/832923/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Start with the vocabulary, because this item is unreadable without it.  When somebody asks ChatGPT or Gemini or Perplexity which moisturiser to buy, the answer often points at specific sources, and one of those sources is increasingly a YouTube video.  The industry calls that being cited.  It is not a view, it is not a click and it is not a recommendation in a feed.  It is closer to what a reference librarian does when they hand you one book out of a shelf of forty.",
        "Tuesday's news hook is the smallest part of this.  Net Influencer wrote up an analysis by Sundogs, a company that scores video advertising creative, using <mark>400 AI-cited videos</mark> supplied by the agency Jellyfish from its tracking platform, checked against 3,500 non-cited videos from the same creators, across United States consumer packaged goods and automotive.  Its source mix of cited videos: roughly 60 percent creators, 30 percent editorial, 10 percent brands.  Treat that one with care and read the flagnote — there is no published paper behind it.",
        "The number worth your attention is Jellyfish's own, and it has been public since June.  Across <mark>27 million AI model responses</mark> covering ten industries, seven large language models and more than fifteen markets, Jellyfish reports YouTube creator content appearing in more than one in four AI answers, nearly one in two in high-intent sectors like consumer electronics and financial services, and this: in fast-moving consumer goods, <mark>nine out of ten YouTube videos surfaced by the models came from independent creators, not brands</mark>.  Jack Smyth, who runs Brandtech in Australia and helped build the tool: \"AI models are becoming the new gatekeepers of discovery\".",
        "Then the mechanism, which comes from the biggest dataset of the three.  OtterlyAI published a study on 2 March covering <mark>over 100 million AI citation instances</mark> across six engines in a 30-day window, and it ran correlations against every piece of video metadata it could reach.  A correlation runs from 1, a perfect relationship, down to 0, none at all.  Views against how often a video gets cited: <mark>r = -0.03</mark>.  Likes: -0.02.  Channel subscribers: -0.03.  Not weak.  Zero.  Meanwhile <mark>40.83 percent of cited videos had fewer than 1,000 views</mark>, 36 percent had fewer than 15 likes, and 35 percent of cited channels had fewer than 10,000 subscribers.",
        "Two things did correlate, and both are decisions somebody makes after the edit is locked.  Description length came in at <mark>r = 0.31</mark>, the strongest signal in the entire set and well ahead of recency at about 0.3.  And timestamps: 31 percent of cited videos carried timestamp signals, either a timestamped citation or chapter markers in the description, and <mark>78 percent of those were cited more than once</mark>, most often across two to five separate chapters.  One video, five citations, because it was legible in sections.  OtterlyAI's own summary of the behaviour is the cleanest sentence anyone has written about this: \"AI citation behavior looks less like 'recommendation' and more like 'reference selection'\".",
        "Length is the other hard split.  Long-form took <mark>94 percent</mark> of YouTube citations against 5.7 percent for Shorts, with playlists, channels and livestreams making up the remaining 0.3.  Sundogs reports the same direction from the other end — cited videos commonly run eight minutes or longer, and Ben Jones, its founder and chief executive, says the engines do not reward reputation either: \"A great creator who published a lot of videos on a topic did not get cited more than an AI content farm in Kenya\".",
        "And on Monday this stopped being somebody else's surface.  Social Media Today reports that Ask YouTube, YouTube's conversational search, is now available to all United States users and has reached the mobile app, so it now runs on desktop, mobile and television.  YouTube says it \"allows users to ask complex queries, refine with follow-up questions\".  Nothing has been published about how it sources or ranks anything, or whether advertising will appear in it.  But the question a shopper used to type into a search box is now being typed into a box on the platform where your video already lives.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("9 in 10", "YouTube videos AI models surfaced in consumer goods came from creators, not brands"),
        ("-0.03", "correlation between a video's view count and how often an engine cites it"),
        ("94%", "of YouTube citations went to long-form, against 5.7 percent to Shorts"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Everybody here sells the remedy.  Sundogs sells creative scoring for video advertising, Jellyfish sells AI-visibility consulting and the tracking tool the data came from, and OtterlyAI sells AI search monitoring; Net Influencer disclosed none of this.  The Sundogs analysis has no published artefact at all — Net Influencer says it was published in July 2026, it is not on Sundogs' own site, and no other outlet has covered it, so none of its figures can be checked.  Two independent reviews of this literature state plainly that no published study has broken a large citation sample down by creator against brand channel, which makes the 60 / 30 / 10 split one vendor's unpublished count of 400 videos rather than an industry finding.  The nine-in-ten figure is Jellyfish's own, measured with its own tool, and dates from 25 June.  OtterlyAI analysed only videos already cited inside its window, so its correlations explain repeat citation rather than how a video gets picked up in the first place, and it says so itself.  Two further figures in the Sundogs write-up — 11 billion dollars moving out of search optimisation, and video being 80,000 to 100,000 times richer than text — are Ben Jones's own estimates with no source attached, and are not used here.",
    "so_what": "An engine choosing a source is not running a popularity contest, and the correlations say so about as flatly as a number can.  It is looking for something it can quote in sections, which is why length, chapter structure and a description that actually describes the contents beat every audience metric you report on.  That has two consequences for a brand.  The first is that your owned channel is roughly a tenth of what gets surfaced in consumer goods, and you cannot buy the other nine tenths, because the slot is not for sale.  The second is cheerier: the strongest single signal anybody has measured is the description box, which is free, which you control completely, and which on most brand channels is a link tree and a boilerplate paragraph.",
    "do_this": "Pick the ten videos on your own channel that answer a real buying question and rewrite their descriptions this week as a genuine written account of what the video contains, with chapter timestamps for every section, then watch whether they start appearing as sources.  Brief your next creator partnership as a long-form comparison that names competing products, because that is what gets quoted, and set the deliverable at eight minutes or more rather than a Short.  And when you next report on creator work, put the evergreen videos in a separate line from the launch-window ones, since recency correlates at about 0.3 and views at zero.",
}

